From the code shown, I can spot a likely issue. In your `Resume` implementation, you're using `size_full()` on both the root div and the inner "resume" div. The `size_full()` method typically sets both height and width to 100%, which means your scrollable container is constrained to the viewport height.

Try modifying the inner "resume" div to use `w_full()` instead of `size_full()`, so it can expand vertically beyond the viewport height while maintaining full width:

```rust
div()
    .id("resume")
    .w_full()  // Changed from size_full()
    .overflow_y_scroll()
    .overflow_x_hidden()
    .flex()
    .flex_col()
    .gap_4()
    // ... rest of the children
```

This should allow the content to scroll properly since the container won't be constrained to the viewport height anymore. The outer root div can keep `size_full()` since it should match the viewport dimensions.

If this doesn't resolve the issue, then yes, it might be helpful to see how GPUI implements these layout primitives, particularly how it handles overflow and sizing constraints.
